Date: Tue, 25 May 93 00:47:24 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Is AI's failure evidence for the existence of souls?

Mark_Muhlestein writes:

>It seems to me that David has a valid point, but that the result is
>very weak.  The reason for this is that in a Bayesian analysis, as I
>understand it, it is normally considered correct to assign equal
>probabilities to all unknown components, the sum adding up to one.

No. That does not work because the procedure you describe is not well
defined. Suppose, for example, we are considering a variable X that has an
unknown value between 0 and 1. The equivalent of the procedure you describe
would be to assume a uniform probability density. But that gives a
non-uniform density for X squared--about whose distribution we also know
nothing.

Bayesian analysis, as I understand it, starts with some set of prior
probabilities based on whatever information you already have and describes
how to modify them on the basis of further information. I do not think one
can make sense out of a zero knowledge starting point--that is one of the
problem with Rawls.

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


