Date: Tue, 25 May 1993 01:46:16 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

Hal Finney:
> Once people are
> out there, if they decide that they need "sunlight rights" or "periastron
> slots" or whatever else it turns out is natural, they can create these
> with court systems much as we create rights to quiet, unobstructed views,
> or clean air today.

I wonder, though, how many people in our legal system (courts,
arbiters, lawyers in general) are versed sufficiently in
the economics of homesteading to to (a) even have a property-rights 
solution cross their minds, instead of an absolute legal ruling or 
government-regulated bureaucracy like the FCC, and (b) create a flexible, 
low-transaction cost, and reasonably fair form of property?  When was the 
last time any court system created a new set of property rights to 
resolve a dispute?  

Those interested in a contemporary dispute along these lines can
check out the current debate in sci.space between astronomers and
some folks in Georgia who want to launch a giant billboard into
orbit.  The American Physical Society (APS) made a predictable
rant against "setting a precedent for the unregulated commercialization
of space".  Horrors!  One poster suggested something along the lines I 
posted about a few months ago on this list, namely creating a fungible 
"dark sky" property right, measured in physical units of illumination.
This would be a right to transmit or reflect photons towards Earth's
night side, similar to the SO2 pollution rights now being traded 
between utilities.  Who are the homesteaders in this case, ie
who uses the dark sky, is kind of a judgement call, but there
has to be some measurable commitment, not just an expressed desire
for the rights.  Distributing fungible shares proportional to investment 
of persons or organizations in ground-based telescope equipment was 
suggested.  That would make telescope makers happy anyway :-), happy
enough they would probably underwrite the distribution of dark sky
shares to their customers.

A danger here is that a "disenfranchised" party, eg a casual 
stargazer, billboard-hater, or capitalist-basher might prefer to
spend their money coercing other people via the legal system instead
of buying and holding shares for preservation.  (I've heard rumors
that some environmental group is buying up SO2 emission rights and
sitting on them, is that true?).  Given the economic illiteracy
of the court system, they would probably win in several jurisdictions.

An interesting point that came up in  response is that
under the "Freedom of the Skies" treaty, no nation can claim 
sovereignty of any resource beyond the Earth's atmosphere.  Outside the 
bounds of a few other outer space treaties that are either legally meaningless 
("space is the common heritage of mankind") or narrow (guidelines and
bureaucracy for the allocation of Clarke orbit slots & frequencies),
outer space is an anarchy, like the Internet.  Alas, given the 
dominance of government bureaucracies in the utilization of space,
especially the ongoing military importance of space, I doubt it
will stay that way for long.

