Date: Tue, 25 May 1993 06:52:37 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: META: Chaitin & a deconstruction of misunderstanding

Q."Can't we all just get along?"  
(A. Who do you think we are, posthumans or something? :-)

There are way too many gratuitous recriminations flying around on this
thread, so I'll add some of my own. :-)  A couple posters criticized 
too strongly on points they were not sufficiently well versed in.  
The dismissal of their criticisms with "RTFM" similarly fell short of 
good communications.  I can say as a computer scientist
that some of the criticisms, though worded too strongly,
were reasonable, and some of them were interesting even if not 
accurate.

First of all, the criticism of Chaitin's use of "random" and
"computable" in popular venues is reasonable.  These are terms of 
art that do not mean the same thing in CS theory as they do in the 
vernacular, and as such their special meaning should be explained when 
addressing a general audience, both in Scientific American and here on 
this list.  Criticism resulting from insufficient explanation of 
advanced topics should be both made and handled gently, whether
the field is computer science, physics, economics, or philosophy.
We have seen needless flames stemming from obscure terms of art used from
all four of these fields on this list, with the accompanying 
expectation that non-specialists should automatically be able to
distinguish the term of art from the vernacular.  There may even
be the assumption by some specialists that the vernacular definition
is no longer useful; this is especially pernicious.

The vernacular definition Michael Price may have been using
for "computable" is "capable of being run on a machine."
One of the main topics on this list is whether the human mind can
be run on a machine (uploading).  The assumption that machines can 
only run computable problems is a *hypothesis*, not a proven
mathematical fact.  Furthermore, Chaitin himself violates the CS
definition of "computable" when he states that "at the limit, Omega 
becomes a computable sequence."  This whole confusion about the word
"computable" can be occasion for asking deeper questions
about the theory of computability and its relevence to uploading etc.,
rather than "I'm right and you're wrong because I RTFM" posturing.

Chaitin's use of "random" to mean "algorithmically incompressible"
is even more obscure; it's part of his own theory, not traditional
mainstream CS.  

We need to be reasonable about what we can expect out of this
forum's readers.  We are are a pretty smart bunch.  But we do not, 
for the most part, share detailed specialties to the point where
we understand most of the obscure jargon in each other's fields.  If
your main interest is mainstream computer science theory, you
may learn more by reading the textbooks and asking questions or dicussing
in comp.theory and whatever specialized mailing lists exist.
If you want to talk to this list's readers about your field of interest,
you'll have better luck if you take some time to explain points of
confusion than you will by posting a required reading list
fit for a semester-long grad school class.  If you insist on
such reading you should also offer course credit. :-)

This forum is mainly interested in how that and many other theories
can help answer questions such as "how complex is a self-replicating 
machine", "can the human mind run on a machine", "how to extend
life", "how to create freedom", etc.  Note that we often use the word 
"computer" instead of "machine", but given the confusion over what 
constitutes "computability", the questionable scope of the Church-Turing 
thesis, etc. it behooves us to be both careful and open-minded on this 
point.

BTW, (ab)use of the verncacular does not escape the field of 
computer theory.  Theorists often use vaguely physical terms, as in "very 
strong results" and "machine A is more powerful than B".  Such abuse 
is often a channel marker indicating said theoretician has reached 
the explanatory limits of the theory, or at least their understanding 
of it.  But rather than flaming such statements, I see them as an opportunity
to probe and test the theory.  For example, "powerful" sometimes
means "more concise" or "explains more observations" within a certain 
framework of assumptions, and questioning the scope of the
theory in such terms can be pathway to something interesting.

At the risk of contradicting myself, I also agree with the poster
who pointed out that flames can be *good*, in the sense that we
have the opportunity here to be more honest and straightforward
without a lot of the posturing and smalltalk that goes on in
face-to-face contact.  When flames are used for posturing
we lose that capability.  We should however take advantage
of this abstract medium to be honest, straightforward, and even somewhat
carefree with our opinions about things and ideas, and to not react 
personally or emotionally when others honestly disagree with the
ideas we believe.

