Subject: New Brain Test: "Einstein einstein.... Penrose!"

I will respond to Mr. Fraering's thoughts about brain representation
in a computer.  From my understanding of how the brain functions,
I believe that it can be modelled thus:

Each neuron is represented by a list of data.  One part is an
equation describing when the neuron will fire in response to
incoming signals.  There will be a set of state variables that
cover time since last firing, and a recent firing history.
There is a table of pulses in progress and their positions on
the neuron, which is described by a tree-type mapping.  The
synapse connections to other neurons are another table.

For a neuron with 10,000 synapse connections, and 100 billion
neurons in the brain, you need on the order of 50 bits per
synapse to record an arbitary connectivity.  Allow for another
50 bits to decribe the state of the synapse (whether it is
excitory or inhibitory, the strength of the connection, depletion
level of neurotransmitters, etc.).  So we have 1 million bits
per neuron synapse table.  Allow another 1 million bits to describe
the shape of the neuron dendrites, and the other neuron equations
and variables.  So for the brain model as a whole we would
need 2x10^17 bits to describe the complete mapping.  In common
computer terms this is 25,000 Terabytes of data.

The typical neuron firing rate is 100 Hz, so we can estimate that
the brain model must be updated at about that rate.  The connectivity
map will not be redrawn each cycle, but the pulses will propagate.
If we assume we are tracking a pulse down every dendritic branch,
then we might be tracking 1000 pulse positions per neuron, at
10 bits position resolution, at 100 Hz.  So we need 1 million bits/sec
per neuron, or 10^17 bits/sec for the full brain model.

>From the above estimates, I can draw some conclusions.  One is that
until now, all attempts at AI have been hopelessly underpowered,
even if intelligence was several orders of magnitude simpler to
model than the brain hardware itself.  Second, given about a million-
fold improvement in computers, we will have machines that can
model the brain at full fidelity.  The caveat is that there is
not any hidden processing going on in neurons at a level below
the synapses/pulses we see now.

I claim that whether the computer implementing the brain model
is parallel or serial is irrelevant.  What is relevant is that the
MODEL of the brain correctly describes the parallelism inherent in
the biological structures.  The choice of a parallel vs. serial
machine should be made on the usual bases of cost and ability
to manage the processing job.  I expect as a practical engineering
matter, that massively parallel machines will reach the required
speed and data capacity first.

So, to sum up, I believe that AI has failed so far for the
same reason that reaching orbit by manually throwing sticks in the
air fails: grossly inadequate power for the job at hand.

Dani Eder

