Date: Tue, 25 May 1993 12:20:47 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul? 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

I'd like to open this by noting that I have the greatest respect for
David, and that this is not a flame, just an honest intellectual
disagreement. I hope no one gets hard feelings from it.

The substance of this article is a refutation of the claim by Dr.
Friedman that that there is no greater reason to believe in a
mechanistic mind than in a non-mechanistic one.

ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu says:
> 2. I do, however, have a prejudice against treating a guess as a fact,
> interpreting all further evidence with that fact as a given, and then
> claiming that the evidence so interpreted supports the fact. So far as
> consciousness is concerned, it seems to me that the mechanistic
> version--essentially the idea that I am a computer program running on my
> brain--is only a guess.

I would disagree.

Here is my evidence.

To begin with, I think the "computer program" model is wrong. I
believe the brain is mechanistic, and thus modelable with a computer
program, but I don't see any evidence of a "program" being there.
(This is not to say that you can't simulate the brain with a program
-- but it isn't architected that way.)

However, we can take a nice reductionist view that shows that what we
are dealing with is mechanistic.

1) We see the effects of other people's minds in operation only via
   their operation of their musculature and similar organ systems.

2) Detailed study has shown that muscles seem, in virtually all cases,
   to contract only in response to nerve impulses, and that they
   operate consistently in the presense of such impulses. Nerve
   impulses have both been observed and generated, and the results are
   all consistant with theory.

3) Following the path backwards, nerve cells are consistantly studied
   to fire only in response to inputs from sensory organs (or similar
   such inputs) or from other nerve cells. Again, this interaction has
   been thoroughly studied.

4) The operation of nerve cells has been extensively studied, and a
   very good model of how they function has been produced. We
   understand fairly well how nerve cells are triggered (via
   neurotransmitters bridging synapses), how their signals are
   transmitted down their length (the various ion pump mechanisms and
   change in membrane permeability), and how their signals are then
   transmitted along to the next cell in the line. At no time have
   interactions inconsistant with the known mechanistic operation been
   observed.

5) Nerve cells have been extensively studied interacting with each
   other in the brain. Again, the operations all are consistant with a
   mechanistic system -- all observations yield positive evidence for
   a fairly good (although still admittedly incomplete) model of nerve
   cell interaction.

So, summarizing thus far, we have nerve cells examined in vitro and in
vivo, interacting with each other and with other kinds of cells, and
what we find is consistant positive evidence for a mechanistic system,
without any observation of extranatural phenomena.

Now, as I have already noted, the operation of the brain has also been
extensivly mapped. There are locations who's excision produces
consistant deficits in function. There are locations who's stimulation
with current produces consistant phenomena such as visual artifacts,
motor activity, memory stimulation, etc. There have also not been any
discoveries of phenomena not consistant with mechanistic operation of
the mind. In short, everything thus far seems to integrate in with the
mechanistic explanation.

We have studied, and what we see are mechanistic nerve cells taking
sensory inputs, channelling them through other mechanistic nerve cells
throughout the brain, and mechanistically generated outputs going
mechanistically via other nerve cells to control various mechanisms
throughout the body. (I simplify slightly, ignoring the effects of
hormones, neurotransmitters and the compounds that interfere with
them, etc -- but all those mechanisms are mechanistic too.)  We can't
yet fully explain how the jumble of nerves interconnected just so
produces the complex behavior we see in humans, but we can pretty much
trace every step of the process and we see only mechanistic phenomena.
The whole is extremely complex -- but we can trace all the parts and
how they fit to each other and all we get is mechanistic phenomena.

Now, where could a "soul" hide in all this? I can't see a space for
it. Presumably, a "soul" would have to alter the behavior of the brain
in some manner, that is, it would have to interfere with the well
understood mechanistic activity -- but we don't see such distortion
occur, and not for want of observation.

> Nobody I have talked to has a convincing story of
> how the most fundamental fact I know--the subjective experience of being
> conscious--is explained by a mechanism.

I will fully agree that we do not yet have a convincing explanation of
how this happens. However, this is not an unusual situation in
science. For instance, radioactivity was a well known phenomenon for a
very long time before anyone produced a good explanation for it -- but
the phenomenon was very easily shown to operate according to well
structured models. Similarly for many other phenomena.

> Nobody has built a mechanism that
> acts as much like a conscious being as other people do.

Thats true, but on the other hand, we've found that we can
mechanistically and consistantly interfere with consciousness, and we
have also observed that the components of the brain work in a purely
mechanistic manner, with no evidence that their operation is
interfered with by external extranatural mechanism ("the soul").

> Of course, the various non-mechanism theories do no better.

Actually, they do far far worse. I have actual evidence against them
in the form of the consistant lack of apparent interference in the
function of neurons by non-mechanistic entities.

> Nobody has
> offered me an adequate nonmechanistic explanation of consciousness, and I
> have not met any golems yet. We do have a method of producing conscious
> beings, but it (fortunately) does not depend on our understanding how they
> work.

I somewhat suspect that the first real AIs will work by a similar
mechanism in so far as they will be slavish copies of the human brain
cast in computer software. Understanding may take quite a long time --
but engineers have often shown an ability to thoroughly exploit
phenomena that are poorly understood.

> My conclusion is that I do not understand the nature of consciousness. That
> being so both the possibility of uploading and the existence of an immortal
> soul are possibilities, neither is more.

I cannot more strongly disagree. Uploading only implies that the brain
is mechanistic -- and there is extremely strong evidence that it is.
An "immortal soul" would require that the brain be non-mechanistic,
which is flatly contradicted by the evidence. In the last century of
studying the brain we have yet to observe a "ghost" pulling the
strings of the machine -- it all seems to be just machine. It strikes
me as exceptionally unlikely that a non-mechanistic system is involved.

> 3. It seems to me that a lot of talk about what is or is not "proved by
> science" has an element of "heads I win, tails you lose." I am fairly sure
> that people who believe in a mechanism explanation would have taken success
> of AI (say a computer that acted as conscious as a cat) as strong evidence
> in favor of their theory. It follows that failure is evidence
> against.

No. Not at all. The converse is not the same as the contrapositive --
even small children learning geometry know this. If the proposition

   If we can build AIs today, then intelligence can be modeled in a
   mechanistic way

is true, we can say only

   If we cannot model intelligence in a mechanistic way then we cannot
   build AIs today.

which is the contrapositive. We cannot say

   If we cannot build AIs today then we cannot model intelligence in a
   mechanistic way.

Which is the converse, and as we all know, the converse of a
proposition is NOT always, or even usually, true.

> Many
> people are unwilling to accept that because they assign a probability of
> one to their explanation of consciousness--which means that any failure
> must be assigned to some other cause.

I am unwilling to accept it because I have both direct evidence of the
mechanistic nature of the behavior of humans and because I have pretty
direct evidence of how misguided AI research thus far has been. I am
NOT rationalizing.

> One can see the same phenomenon in
> Paul Ehrlich et. al., refusing to lower their probability of doom when
> particular predictions fail to come true.

But this is specious. First, Erlich and company really had no good
evidence for their positions -- whereas there is direct and
extraordinarily solid evidence that neural function is wholly
mechanistic. Second of all, we are talking about ENGINEERING, not
SCIENCE. As with Da Vinci and Babbage, what we have is engineers being
orders of magnitude too optimistic about what they can do with the
technology of their time. Even the most optimistic estimates of human
computing capacity say that we will not achive human level capacity in
our computers for another three decades! This is not a rationalization
-- this is a solid and easy to observe fact! AI researchers expected
to be able to get computers to do human like things and we don't even
really have the capacity to simulate INSECTS, let alone humans!

I will also note that, judging purely by our accomplishments in AI, we
actually HAVE made some stunning progress of late when we have stuck
to what we can reasonably hope to simulate. I refer most specifically
to Rod Brooks and his work on subsumption architecture based robots.
His creations, which are substantially less complex in their neural
structure than insects, behave strikingly like "live" organisms. Given
our current level of compute power available, this should be more or
less the level we would expect to have success at.

> 4. Much of this goes back to an argument I had with Minski, who was
> claiming that he knew the mind was a program because that was proved by the
> gedanken experiment of uploading one neuron at a time (most people on the
> list are probably familiar with the argument). My response was that he had
> not proved his result because he had not done the experiment--he was merely
> concluding that it would go the way he thought because he already knew the
> right answer, and then taking the fact that it would go that way as
> evidence that his answer was right.

Well, you do have me there. I can't prove that AI is possible yet
because I can't yet do it. On the other hand, I'd say that Goddard was
perfectly sane and had all the evidence in his court when, fifty years
before it was possible to reach the moon, he showed that all the
theory showed it could be done. Similarly, I'd say that the AI
researchers of the 1960s were all perfectly sane -- just sixty or
eighty years too early.

I'll point out that this is NOT a position held without evidence. It
has plenty of evidence for it -- the consistant and continued
observation of a mechanistic brain seems to be all but proof of the
assertion at this point. Unless you can give me an explanation of how
a soul could possibly interact with the known mechanistic processes in
such a way as to evade detection by researchers for all these years of
the phenomenon, I'd have to argue that you are wrong.

Perry


